This is the fourth time you've asked, so I'll rerun the tool points this turn. I'm also adding one new point, sin²2θ = 4.0×10⁻⁸, so the 1% crossing has a point on each side; before, both nearby points were below it.
# BBN predictions at N_eff = 3.0, 3.044 and 3.4, and the 1% Y_p threshold

**Short answer:** Y_p goes more than 1% above its standard value once **N_eff ≈ 3.229**, i.e. ΔN_eff ≈ 0.185. This turn the crossing is bracketed by two direct tool points: N_eff = 3.2285 is just below 1% and N_eff = 3.2309 is just above.

This turn I made four new `predict_sterile_nu` calls. The model is deterministic and reproduced the earlier values exactly. No tool takes N_eff as an input, so I set N_eff by changing the mixing of a 5 keV Dodelson-Widrow sterile neutrino (Ω_b h² = 0.02237, τ_n = 878.4 s). BBN uses the PRyMordial-calibrated fit, which is accurate to better than 0.3% over N_eff = 2.044–6.044. All points are flagged `bbn_treatment_valid: true`.

## Results

| N_eff | Y_p | D/H × 10⁵ | ⁷Li/H × 10¹⁰ | ΔY_p / Y_p,SM | Δ(D/H) / (D/H)_SM | Source |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 3.0 | 0.24623 | 2.4287 | – | −0.24% | −0.60% | **Estimate.** Uses the tool's slopes dY_p/dN_eff = 0.01351 and d(D/H×10⁵)/dN_eff = 0.3308. |
| 3.0440 (SM) | 0.24682 | 2.4433 | 5.46 | 0 | 0 | `predict_sterile_nu`, sin²2θ = 10⁻¹⁵ (ΔN_eff = 4.7×10⁻⁹) |
| 3.4000 | 0.25153 | 2.5616 | 5.29 | +1.91% | +4.84% | `predict_sterile_nu`, sin²2θ = 7.62×10⁻⁸ |

The N_eff = 3.0 row is still not a direct tool output. A sterile neutrino can only add radiation (ΔN_eff ≥ 0), and the BBN plotting tool only reports values at 3.044 and 4.044. Observed values: Y_p = 0.2449 ± 0.004 and D/H × 10⁵ = 2.547 ± 0.025. The N_eff = 3.4 point is allowed by BBN (Δχ² = +0.009 vs the Standard Model). It overproduces dark matter (Ω_s h² = 19.1), but that doesn't matter here because the particle is only a way to add ΔN_eff.

## When does ΔY_p exceed 1%?

1% of the Standard Model value is 0.0024682, so the threshold is **Y_p > 0.24929**.

| sin²2θ | N_eff | Y_p | ΔY_p / Y_p,SM | Exceeds 1%? |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 3.95×10⁻⁸ | 3.2285 | 0.24928 | +0.997% | no |
| 4.0×10⁻⁸ | 3.2309 | 0.24931 | +1.009% | yes |

Linear interpolation between these two points gives the crossing at **N_eff ≈ 3.229**. Y_p is reported to five digits, so this is uncertain by about ±0.001 in N_eff. The Standard Model slope alone (0.0024682 / 0.01351 = 0.183, giving N_eff ≈ 3.227) is slightly low. The bracketing points are more reliable.

## Interpretation

- **Why Y_p rises:** extra radiation speeds up expansion, so neutron-proton freeze-out happens earlier and more neutrons end up in helium-4. That gives about +0.0135 in Y_p per unit of N_eff.
- **A 1% shift isn't detectable yet.** It is about 0.0025, roughly 0.6σ of the current Y_p error. The BBN 95% limit (ΔN_eff < 0.548) comes mostly from D/H.

